From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(mining): reconcile completed and creative block breaks

Send MineBlock requests or legacy destroy transactions in PLAYER_AUTH_INPUT at the break tick, including the matching input flags. Predict damage for tagged vanilla tools and accept authoritative durability only for the same held stack. Route creative breaks through Bedrock player actions, suppress redundant mining swings and retain authoritative correction for instant breaks.

For server-authoritative Survival, emit Continue/Predict with the matching MineBlock request. A negotiated add-on reports held-target admission before Java mining callbacks, including the miss/release boundary that Java loses after completion clears its local active flag. Retain the completed native gesture while held attack still admits a target. Map the next Java Start to Continue, without an intervening Abort or a new native Start.

Native Windows 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 retains that gesture across contiguous stone blocks and uses a 250 ms inter-block deadline. Original REA constructor/continuation evidence and bounded live observation ev_b94b026ed1f8594aba3eb27938386f18ee34bd01983203bff8d3795e69ddfc9f establish the field, policy and target transition. The add-on owns that local timed admission; this core owns request and input-frame ordering. Debugger observations establish values and order, not uninstrumented latency.

A miss, release or fresh press closes a retained completed target with one Abort. Hold a fresh Start until that Abort frame has serialized, retaining pending direction changes and cancellation. Earlier native restoration controls include a miss; they do not establish that every completion closes the gesture. Unnegotiated clients retain the completion-close fallback because ordinary Java no longer reports a release after clearing its active flag. The legacy client-authoritative sequence and creative path keep their existing timing.

Activate retention only after a valid versioned serverbound mining-input payload, with the existing input-permission receiver and server-authoritative Survival mode. Fabric serverbound payload registration does not advertise a clientbound receiver for the same channel; ChannelStorage is not an acknowledgement. Channel removal disables retention and closes a completed gesture.

A real custom-payload regression supplies only the advertised permission receiver and verifies held retarget, duplicate cancellation and ordered fresh Start. Existing tests retain the unnegotiated fallback boundary. Installed controls verify the three-block gesture and all MineBlock action contents and durability against native; a separate rapid-repress target/input phase difference remains open. These cases do not establish general mining parity or all instant-break admission.

